WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.660
Film Courage: Knowing that you love these
characters with edges how did you make them

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.820
still make them redeemable, likable in some
way.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.410
Maybe they are not even likable but somehow
the audience is not going to give up on them?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.830
Orson Oblowitz, Filmmaker/Photographer: Because
they are humans, they are just like you and

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:17.400
me, that’s the point.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:24.110
If you can just get the point across that
these people all sleep, they all eat, they

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:30.009
all breathe oxygen, if you can just get that
little point across I think we can all relate

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:31.009
to that.

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:34.750
It’s so silly but I’ve always thought
about that because I do like these characters

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:42.900
that are a little off, that sometimes do make
bad decisions instead of good decision but

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.890
I think if you also show them they’re humans,
they struggle with so many of the same things

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:48.890
we struggle with.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:52.250
How they are going to pay their rent they
struggle with, how did the parents love them,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.080
they struggle with all of these very human
factors.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:03.010
If you can just show a little of that I think
we can’t help but relate to them.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:10.560
I try to show people also small details in
their personal life.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.130
A lot of this new movie is a guy sitting by
himself in a room eating and watching TV because

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.990
we all sit in a room and eat.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.090
We all sit by ourselves and watch.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.240
We all have trouble sleeping, well all have
nightmares.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.500
These little things so I think that’s how
you just humanize them through reality.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:37.380
Also in your more surreal movie you kind of
take that approach where you show there’s

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:43.970
a certain humanity behind that bad decision
making, behind the kind of acting in bad faith

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.030
type of decisions.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:52.290
That’s it for me, if you can just get the
point just for a moment across you can get

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.740
away with whatever you want.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.430
There are a lot of irrepressible characters
in cinema that I love.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.430
BAD LIEUTENANT, right?

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:10.170
BAD LIEUTENANT’s Harvey Keitel is the great
example of it in that film but he tries to

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:16.040
at one point he really feels like he’s making
the right decision and you have to show that.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:21.690
You show the human side to the character and
I think we all will be willing to go for the

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.270
ride if it’s also entertaining.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.760
That’s a big part of it as well.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.760
Film Courage: I’d love to see more female
characters like that because Author Margaret

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:38.790
Atwood has a great quote and she says women
are either painted as total angels or as horrible

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.150
human beings but they’re human, so there’s
a spectrum.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.830
Whereas it feels like sometimes men can have
more of a gray area but I wish it would show

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:52.180
they don’t have to have such clearly defined
roles…either she’s a bad character or

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.040
she’s great but she can’t be anything
in-between.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.270
Orson: Well that was my goal with “Queen.”

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:56.270
[Orson’s film QUEEN OF HOLLYWOOD BLVD).

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.200
I wanted to literally take a character that
we were so accustomed with the James Bond’s

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.890
or Robert Mitchum’s or Steve McQueen’s
or any of these great male icons that we’ve

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:11.180
had throughout the years and allow a woman
to make those same mistakes.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:16.520
Allow her to be able to live in that gray
area that you’re talking about.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.209
That was really my goal was to take all these
characters and say why not let a woman be

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:22.209
it.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:26.069
You know what…you should have heard some
of the irreprehensible things I’ve written

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:32.851
on the Internet about her and it’s because
it’s unfamiliar.

00:03:32.851 --> 00:03:38.680
We are in this world where we are so obsessed
with man and woman and women are a lot of

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.540
times relegated to these…either they are
these terrible evil you know the woman who

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:52.769
is the psycho crazy woman who destroys everything
or she is the angel, love interest savior

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:55.530
character and there ain’t no difference.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.690
We are all trying to figure this life thing
out together why can’t a woman make the

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.340
same decisions a man make’s.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:07.330
It’s crazy that the media has sold us this
lie for so long.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:12.060
So Queen one hundred percent and I hope I
landed there.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:20.069
I’m not sure if I did but I tried to let
her live in this morally gray area and I would

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:21.250
see how it goes.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.879
Film Courage: And these were comments underneath
the trailer or…?

00:04:24.879 --> 00:04:29.160
Orson: Just the different comments in the
trailer or things I’ve heard people say

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:36.550
to my face, friends who didn’t even realize
they were kind of saying something that was

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:45.979
beyond…not misogynistic…it’s just unaware
about what they are saying about the character.

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:49.409
“She’s so hard,” “She’s such a witchy
witch.”

00:04:49.409 --> 00:04:51.020
And I go really?

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:56.309
Because if James Bond did that you wouldn’t
say that.

00:04:56.309 --> 00:05:06.150
If when Ryan Gosling punches the one actors
in the face in DRIVE no one every asked about

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.150
it.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:14.539
No one ever said…but if she does it, if
she slaps another person suddenly she’s

00:05:14.539 --> 00:05:15.889
evil, sometimes she is cringe-worthy.

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:22.639
We’ve gotten a lot of great reviews but
we’ve had a few people that really went

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:29.669
of the misogynistic train on the film…and
good…that’s what I wanted.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:32.210
I want them to show their face.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.419
Show your face, if that’s what you think,
say it.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:38.400
Film Courage: It’s conversation so then
again you can’t control the outcome of the

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.159
film and it lands where it lands.

00:05:41.159 --> 00:05:41.910
Questions For The Viewers: How did you like
these thoughts on character?

